{"agent":"architecture","findings":[{"id":"arch-001","file":"triggarr/clients/base.py","line":233,"title":"validate_connection now logs raw exception text, bypassing the established _sanitize_exc pattern","category":"pattern-consistency","cwe":null,"severity":"medium","agent_confidence":85,"in_diff":true,"intent_doc_match":null,"problem":"The codebase already has a helper for logging httpx and pydantic exceptions safely: `_sanitize_exc` in triggarr/search/engine.py:30. It is used at 6 log sites (engine.py lines 366, 619, 881, 1138, 1274, 1413). For HTTPStatusError it outputs `HTTP {status_code}`, and for ValidationError it outputs `validation error ({error_count()} issues)`. Those are the same values this diff removes. The diff swaps them for the raw `{exc}`. The helper's docstring says why that is a problem: raw str(exc) 'may contain internal paths, URLs, or API keys that bypass the loguru redacting sink'.","current_code":"logger.warning(
                    "{app}: Unexpected HTTP error: {exc}",
                    app=self._app_name,
                    exc=exc,
                )","fix_hint":"Restore the status_code and error_count fields, or move _sanitize_exc into a shared module (e.g. triggarr/clients/base.py or a utils module) and log exc=_sanitize_exc(exc). Do not import it from search/engine.py: engine already imports clients, so that import would make the two modules depend on each other.","why_it_matters":"The project deliberately logs only a type-based summary of these errors, and this change undoes that in one of the two places it applies. str(HTTPStatusError) includes the full request URL, which reveals the configured *arr host. This module already avoids that on purpose ('Connection refused at configured URL' at line 240).","silenced_marker_nearby":false},{"id":"arch-002","file":"triggarr/clients/base.py","line":252,"title":"ValidationError log now prints full pydantic error text, including echoed response values, instead of a count","category":"pattern-consistency","cwe":null,"severity":"medium","agent_confidence":80,"in_diff":true,"intent_doc_match":null,"problem":"Same pattern break as arch-001, on the pydantic branch. The old line logged `exc.error_count()`, which matches what `_sanitize_exc` does for ValidationError at engine.py:42-43. The new line logs str(exc). In pydantic v2 that includes each failing `input_value`, which here is raw content from the external Radarr/Sonarr/Lidarr response body.","current_code":"logger.warning(
                "{app}: Unexpected API response format: {exc}",
                app=self._app_name,
                exc=exc,
            )","fix_hint":"Log the error count (or the field locations from exc.errors() without input values), consistent with _sanitize_exc.","why_it_matters":"External response content ends up in logs and in the in-memory log buffer the web UI displays. The redacting sink only scrubs known secrets, so anything else in the response passes through.","silenced_marker_nearby":false}],"agent_notes":["The change contradicts the CLAUDE.md Security rule against exposing internal error details. Read literally, that rule is about end users, but triggarr/logging.py keeps a log buffer that the web UI displays, so log text is partly user-facing. Expect overlap with the security agent's findings on the same lines.","The diff adds no new imports, dependencies or import cycles. Abstraction boundaries are unchanged.","Dependency direction: triggarr/search/engine.py imports triggarr.clients.* (lines 21-23). Any shared sanitizer should live in clients or a neutral module, never in search, or it creates a clients -&gt; search -&gt; clients cycle.","I found no tests in tests/ that check the old log strings ('Unexpected HTTP error', 'validation error(s)'), so the change probably breaks no tests. Nothing tests the new wording either.","The API key goes in the X-Api-Key header (base.py:32), not the query string. So str(HTTPStatusError), which prints the URL, most likely exposes the host/path rather than the key. That is why both findings are capped at medium instead of rated as a secret leak."]}
